  "decisions": [
    "Row bands manually reviewed from full sheets and cropped frames; no global grid slicing.",
    "Components ordered by source x; exact counts from immutable contract. No repeated or synthesized poses.",
    "Scale is fixed per measured drawing family/direction, never by individual pose bounds. Native carry and celebration drawings use different proportions.",
    "Feet: midpoint of dark blue boot pixels in bottom 16 percent of core silhouette; ground is exclusive core bottom. Death uses silhouette midpoint to fit horizontal pose.",
    "Retain the principal 8-connected alpha>=32 component plus one pixel of original alpha support. Never harden retained alpha or recolor core.",
    "Raw last action column replaced only by three accepted action-end components, with one shared repair scale.",
    "Nearest-neighbor export preserves hard pixel clusters; original RGBA samples remain untouched before fixed-family resampling.",
    "Compact knockdown replaces all seven death frames at one .239 family scale (standing first pose approximately 110px).",
    "Touching knockdown poses 3/4 separated by a manually reviewed polyline around x1137-1140; pixels assigned once, never deleted or duplicated.",
    "Feet initially target (40,112); extended action poses receive only the minimum horizontal translation required by 1px gutters, logged in reviewed_horizontal_fit_shifts.",
    "All 151 substantial source-core pixels detached by seam partition are retained and assigned to the nearest substantial pose in the same seam zone; exact coverage and nonduplication are tested."
  ],
  "remaining_defects": [
    "Small native body-height/proportion variation survives fixed family scales; no per-frame normalization.",
    "Connected generation edge tint and authored outline remain intentionally; detached flecks alone removed.",
    "Extended action silhouettes require horizontal anchor exceptions to retain full authored poses at fixed scale.",
    "Knockdown poses 3/4 share a touching outline in source; manually assigned seam remains a source-art limitation."
